</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="1c5eba0f-2f29-43d1-b13a-bc342d4f4ae4">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate functions as a restriction on the interest that can be credited to an Index Account, defining the ceiling for returns derived from index performance.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONSTRAINS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="325410d2-10cd-4b16-9227-4b96221af7a2">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate sets a ceiling on earnings for index accounts, whereas a Fixed Account offers a guaranteed return unaffected by such caps or market fluctuations.</data>
  <data key="d4">UNRELATED</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="eec22563-c8f9-4930-abc2-6252103b7b77">
  <data key="d3">The cap rate defines the ceiling for returns, but without the floor rate, participants could face negative credits during poor market periods. Together, they establish bounds on creditable returns.</data>
  <data key="d4">COMPLEMENTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="a3259e6d-b7fd-4e82-92f7-523ed8d8a955">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate limits the maximum possible creditable return, so even if the index performs strongly, only up to this cap applies before considering the Participation Rate’s share.</data>
  <data key="d4">MODIFIES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="42ce1796-4b22-4fa1-987f-341d1746d7f3">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate sets the upper bound on interest credited during each annual period defined by the Point-to-Point Method, limiting potential gains even if the index performs strongly.</data>
  <data key="d4">MODIFIES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="492399d4-a4a8-470a-b04c-a4e38ca316d2">
  <data key="d3">The first sentence establishes the meaning of "Cap Rate" as a ceiling on credited interest. The second sentence illustrates this concept by specifying the exact cap rate applied to the S&amp;P 500 Index Account.</data>
  <data key="d4">EXEMPLIFIES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="e6aa2762-a82e-4371-9d8e-da19a25dd6d1">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate establishes a ceiling on potential returns for indexed accounts, which governs how much interest can be credited during each period. This rule applies specifically to the S&amp;P 500 Index Account described in the second sentence.</data>
  <data key="d4">APPLIES_TO</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="faf66c9e-da0e-4813-9561-2d1480419596">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate limits the maximum interest creditable per period, independent of the Participation Rate, which governs the proportion of gains applied. Together, they define potential returns under specific conditions.</data>
  <data key="d4">MODIFIES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="a0671d34-e31b-4b17-8ed5-6a9d495c5148">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate sets the upper limit on interest credits during a given period; knowing the crediting period is one year clarifies how frequently this ceiling is evaluated and enforced.</data>
  <data key="d4">APPLIES_TO</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="a4c6faf8-57d8-4d84-affb-d94570b3ae60">
  <data key="d3">The cap rate sets the ceiling for potential returns across index accounts, while the floor ensures minimum protection against losses. The S&amp;P 500's zero percent floor aligns with the broader policy design where floors prevent negative credits.</data>
  <data key="d4">COMPLEMENTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="62714aed-4640-40fa-9927-1e7eaaddb24c">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate sets a ceiling on potential returns for indexed accounts, which includes the NASDAQ-100 Index Account described later. This ensures transparency about how much growth can be credited regardless of underlying index performance.</data>
  <data key="d4">APPLIES_TO</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="4144da65-9775-496c-b601-6cbcd31e5849">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate sets the maximum possible interest credit in a period; here, it is applied concretely to the NASDAQ-100 Index Account at 9.0%, illustrating how the general rule operates in practice.</data>
  <data key="d4">EXEMPLIFIES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="32909f74-b806-4169-8ab1-602ea158ef9f">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate sets the upper limit on interest credits, while the Participation Rate determines what portion of the index’s growth is used to calculate those credits. Together, they shape potential returns under the index-based crediting method.</data>
  <data key="d4">MODIFIES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="b0213eb9-bcd3-41f0-9381-ae8e81ac0f24">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate sets the upper limit on interest credited during a given period; knowing the Crediting Period is one year clarifies the timeframe over which this cap operates.</data>
  <data key="d4">APPLIES_TO</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.9</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="bce094ed-5c85-40d1-800b-e0894835715b">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate sets the upper limit for interest credits across index accounts, but it does not influence the specific floor setting for the NASDAQ-100 Index Account, which is independently defined as 0%.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="13cdba53-929e-487b-9dac-c7e31bdd92a0">
  <data key="d3">The definition of Cap Rate establishes a ceiling for interest credits; the Global Diversified Index Account's 8.5% cap serves as a real-world application of this rule.</data>
  <data key="d4">EXEMPLIFIES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="de64be79-d83e-496d-97f6-5d2e80705c21">
  <data key="d3">The first sentence explains the function of the cap rate in determining maximum interest credits. The second sentence identifies a specific index account among several options listed in the document.</data>
  <data key="d4">EXEMPLIFIES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="a5cf74c6-3010-4b87-83b5-452662af8369">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate sets the upper limit on interest credits across all index accounts, but it does not influence or interact with the floor setting of the Global Diversified account, which remains independently defined at 0%.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="22fdc352-4139-456b-bd73-2207bfdefc4e">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate sets the upper limit for interest credits in any period, independent of how much of the index's gain is applied via the Participation Rate. This definition helps contextualize why certain limits exist even when full participation occurs.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.15</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="e35519d3-d4b6-4a91-b9cc-794ecf0231f5">
  <data key="d3">The cap rate governs returns in indexed accounts, but does not influence the maximum allowable policy loan, which depends solely on surrender value. These elements function independently within the policy framework.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="6f98a9e1-688e-4680-b305-6c7ecf379647">
  <data key="d3">The cap rate governs returns on indexed accounts, which may influence loan collateral value over time, but does not affect the timing of loan availability.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="d39b66e7-d38a-450c-a287-bd1bf0a73d22">
  <data key="d3">The cap rate governs potential gains in index-linked accounts, whereas borrowed funds grow at a separate fixed rate in the fixed account. These elements operate independently within the policy’s financial framework.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="cdffaf84-8d6b-4f83-8142-43784e03289a">
  <data key="d3">The cap rate influences potential earnings in index accounts, but does not affect how policy loans impact the death benefit. These two elements function separately within the policy’s economic framework.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="024598e4-2394-4fad-b636-543f3ffb9225">
  <data key="d3">The cap rate limits potential gains in index accounts, which could influence long-term accumulation and thus indirectly affect the basis for partial withdrawals under Option 1, though not directly tied to withdrawal impact on death benefit.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.15</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="16db743e-8a3d-4763-8527-b5d06fd8aafb">
  <data key="d3">The cap rate limits upside gains in index-linked accounts, which may indirectly affect the attractiveness of taking policy loans when compared to alternative investment returns. Conversely, the 1% net loan cost reflects a stable funding mechanism regardless of index performance ceilings.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDIRECTLY_RELATED</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.35</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate sets a ceiling on earnings from index-linked accounts, which could influence whether policyholders opt for loans instead of withdrawing funds, especially given the fixed 4% loan rate. This creates a subtle economic trade-off between investing in high-cap-indexed accounts versus borrowing against cash value.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.15</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="326a19f6-3d06-4cde-a9f0-0b1923801873">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 establishes the general principle that cap rates set limits on credited returns; fact_sentence_2 illustrates this by specifying the exact cap rate of 8.5% for the Global Diversified Index Account.</data>
  <data key="d4">CORROBORATES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="e56d9b1f-113b-4961-abbd-159d8047aeca">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate sets the ceiling for returns in the Global Diversified Index Account, which otherwise features a 100% participation rate allowing full exposure to index growth—up to the cap threshold.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="665dbf69-ad45-49be-8a49-a811282557e1">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate limits the maximum creditable return based on the index's percentage change, which is determined using the point-to-point method described in fact_sentence_2.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="27176921-2e5c-44aa-bcf0-40ddf6e140f2">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate limits the maximum possible interest credit derived from the index gain and participation rate combination, capping what can be earned even if the underlying index performs strongly.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="f64306b3-60d7-48b6-82c2-6580397642e1">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 establishes the concept of cap limits in indexed accounts; fact_sentence_2 confirms that the Global Diversified account maintains a protective 0% floor, consistent with the risk mitigation features outlined in the overall system.</data>
  <data key="d4">COMPLIMENTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="6ff1f4bc-a2b4-4e15-b514-415d803bd39b">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 specifies the ceiling (cap) on interest credit, which supports and exemplifies the broader claim in fact_sentence_2 that interest is constrained by both a cap and a floor.</data>
  <data key="d4">CORROBORATES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="4392497f-15c1-48ee-8067-43a8245bc89e">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate sets a ceiling on earnings potential in indexed accounts, while surrender charges affect the net proceeds when exiting the policy early. These factors together influence long-term value realization but operate independently in mechanism.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.15</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="01ec375d-e72b-4c13-96a2-da5a23238018">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate limits potential earnings in index accounts, which may affect long-term growth, while the Year 1 surrender charge impacts early withdrawal costs. Though related to overall policy economics, they operate independently without influencing each other directly.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1d4b712e-5e26-4cf9-a7ad-301c683e58e3" target="9dcea0cf-204b-429a-93cc-c257133b7890">
  <data key="d3">The Cap Rate limits potential gains in indexed accounts, whereas the Year 2 surrender charge imposes a cost for early withdrawal. While both affect overall policy value, they operate independently—one governs return generation, the other exit costs.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
