  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual premium cap ensures the policy remains qualified under tax laws. This regulatory constraint has no direct bearing on whether the policyholder earns 0% interest in a given year.</data>
  <data key="d4">NONE</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="50dee1d7-a696-448d-92e6-11dfe14d068f">
  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual cap exists due to tax qualification requirements, which indirectly supports the idea that premium payments are subject to defined limits—even though they may occur at any time.</data>
  <data key="d4">LIMITED_BY</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
