  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual cap maintains tax-qualified status under IRS guidelines, which indirectly supports the feasibility of setting a lower bound like $100 for flexibility.</data>
  <data key="d4">TAX_COMPLIANCE</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="44171d39-27ff-4fcc-bf40-58a3e92080fa">
  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual premium cap exists to maintain tax-qualified status under IRS guidelines, which indirectly supports the feasibility of the $100 minimum as part of a compliant framework.</data>
  <data key="d4">TAX_QUALIFICATION</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="b8046f06-ce88-4546-bb7c-16ba4c69bcf2">
  <data key="d3">Tax qualification rules require that annual contributions stay within certain limits, which is why the policy caps payments at $50,000 per year.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENFORCES_LIMIT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="c0561218-4570-4bd7-925f-25cc01fd0c6d">
  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual premium cap ensures compliance with IRS rules for qualified plans, creating a legal boundary within which financial recommendations operate. This regulatory ceiling provides a framework that makes the suggested $6,000 annual contribution feasible and sustainable under tax-qualified guidelines.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONTEXTUAL_FRAME</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="2bf05a03-d336-46dd-a382-121bb038f1a6">
  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual premium cap ensures compliance with IRS tax qualifications, providing a legal framework within which the recommended $6,000 contribution operates under stable actuarial expectations.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONDITIONS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="45f97aed-091e-480b-b637-f618f4e2486e">
  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual premium cap ensures alignment with IRS tax-deferred savings limits, which indirectly supports long-term policy viability. However, it does not directly influence whether the account value covers monthly fees.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="a6609e69-c5f5-4d6e-9fa1-fc83061e83ae">
  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual premium cap ensures compliance with IRS rules for qualified policies, setting a legal maximum. This limit indirectly supports long-term affordability, enabling policyholders to maintain coverage without exceeding tax-favored thresholds, thus helping them meet conditions under which skipping payments might still preserve benefits.</data>
  <data key="d4">INFLUENCES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.45</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="0d7fabe2-d74d-4b84-a620-42c9acb1c43d">
  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual premium cap ensures alignment with IRS tax-deferred savings limits, maintaining policy eligibility. This structural constraint does not affect how the 3.5% load is calculated on individual payments.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
