  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">CHARGE</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="64f2c791-21a9-461d-84dc-9cd136a92fb5">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">DATE</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="99a2ea02-45ff-4204-9c3f-be85350484db">
  <data key="d3">The monthly COI deduction adjusts according to the spread between the death benefit and account value, reflecting risk exposure. This dynamic charge operates independently of whether the index has declined or been protected by floor safeguards.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="7d57accf-750c-4143-addd-8dbbeb124299">
  <data key="d3">The amount of the monthly cost-of-insurance deduction increases when the account value falls significantly behind the death benefit. Since floor protection guarantees the account won’t go negative, it helps maintain a minimal balance that influences the size of future COI deductions.</data>
  <data key="d4">MODULATES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="116850af-15ce-4679-bcc4-58d5753875d7">
  <data key="d3">The size of the monthly COI deduction, determined by the spread between the death benefit and account value, affects long-term account balance trends, potentially impacting whether the account remains sufficient to maintain benefits under conditions like a 5% index decline.</data>
  <data key="d4">INFLUENCES ACCOUNT VALUE</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.45</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="70667dae-ff8c-4bac-bf04-03807ddb6bd7">
  <data key="d3">The method of determining monthly COI charges—the disparity between the death benefit and account value—affects the overall account performance, thereby reinforcing the importance of maintaining sufficient account value to support long-term protection features like principal preservation.</data>
  <data key="d4">INFLUENCES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="a53ff70e-2b10-4259-9e14-fe7adb0931b1">
  <data key="d3">The amount of monthly COI charged depends on the disparity between the death benefit and current account value, meaning that sustained low account values due to 0% interest could increase future COI charges, though it does not determine the worst-case interest rate itself.</data>
  <data key="d4">MODULATES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="50dee1d7-a696-448d-92e6-11dfe14d068f">
  <data key="d3">The calculation method for the monthly COI deduction has no bearing on when premiums may be paid, as payment timing remains independent of actuarial valuation factors.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="86b72d75-5a59-44c2-b2d6-912ce393cf76">
  <data key="d3">The calculation method for the monthly COI charge, dependent on the difference between the death benefit and account value, has no bearing on whether premiums can be paid at any time during the year.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="7d3930c1-6b23-4a04-93b3-d874c9e7b176">
  <data key="d3">The monthly COI deduction reflects the policyholder’s risk profile relative to the account value and death benefit, influencing overall fund depletion. This does not affect the mandatory $100 floor on premium payments, which remains a standalone condition regardless of COI levels.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="44171d39-27ff-4fcc-bf40-58a3e92080fa">
  <data key="d3">The monthly COI deduction adjusts based on the disparity between the death benefit and account value, meaning consistent premiums like the $100 minimum help maintain sufficient account balance to keep COI costs manageable.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.45</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="b8046f06-ce88-4546-bb7c-16ba4c69bcf2">
  <data key="d3">The formula for calculating monthly COI deductions relies on the difference between the death benefit and account value, independent of the maximum allowable premium amount set at $50,000 per year.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2">
  <data key="d3">The monthly COI deduction adjusts according to the spread between the death benefit and account value, reflecting risk exposure. This mechanism operates independently of the $50,000 annual premium ceiling set for tax qualification purposes.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="c0561218-4570-4bd7-925f-25cc01fd0c6d">
  <data key="d3">The calculation method for monthly COI charges—based on the spread between death benefit and account value—explains why sustaining a higher account value through consistent premiums like $6,000 annually is essential for minimizing ongoing costs.</data>
  <data key="d4">DRIVES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="2bf05a03-d336-46dd-a382-121bb038f1a6">
  <data key="d3">The method for calculating the monthly COI charge—based on the spread between death benefit and account value—is independent of whether current actuarial assumptions hold true. It functions as a fixed formula irrespective of the recommendation’s conditions.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="a6609e69-c5f5-4d6e-9fa1-fc83061e83ae">
  <data key="d3">The cost-of-insurance charge increases when the account value falls below the death benefit, making sustained funding more crucial. As such, maintaining adequate account value allows policyholders to avoid premium payments without risking policy lapse due to insufficient funds.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENABLES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="45f97aed-091e-480b-b637-f618f4e2486e">
  <data key="d3">A higher COI deduction reduces the account value more significantly, increasing the risk of insufficient funds to cover future deductions and potentially preventing the ability to skip premium payments.</data>
  <data key="d4">INFLUENCES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.92</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="0d7fabe2-d74d-4b84-a620-42c9acb1c43d">
  <data key="d3">The monthly COI deduction reflects the risk exposure tied to the policy’s account value, which itself is affected by the initial 3.5% premium load reducing the funds allocated to the account after each payment.</data>
  <data key="d4">INFLUENCED_BY</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="6c580dbf-ec5e-4009-845b-7efb65de3c5a">
  <data key="d3">While the monthly COI deduction reflects risk-based pricing tied to the account's performance relative to the death benefit, the premium load serves broader operational needs unrelated to that specific metric.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.15</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="e4d325d0-3102-4ecf-b8a5-e488e84fa28f">
  <data key="d3">Since the COI is deducted monthly, its amount varies depending on the ongoing comparison between the death benefit and the account value at each billing cycle.</data>
  <data key="d4">MODIFIES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="749f8b25-e9c6-4e2e-a60a-5fdcef1215c4">
  <data key="d3">The monthly COI deduction is determined by the disparity between the death benefit and the account value, and this calculation follows a specific formula involving multiplication by a rate and division by 1,000.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEFINES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.98</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9639c325-d6a7-4181-a801-0a4fdfaefa94" target="6bfb8452-bcec-4db6-b212-fd25bdcfbc79">
  <data key="d3">The cost-of-insurance deduction adjusts according to the disparity between the death benefit and account value, whereas the administrative charge remains constant at $12 monthly regardless of those factors.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
