<edge source="749f8b25-e9c6-4e2e-a60a-5fdcef1215c4" target="99a2ea02-45ff-4204-9c3f-be85350484db">
  <data key="d3">The COI charge is calculated using the death benefit and account value, impacting long-term growth potential. This differs from floor protection, which safeguards against specific market drops.</data>
  <data key="d4">NO_RELATIONSHIP</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="749f8b25-e9c6-4e2e-a60a-5fdcef1215c4" target="7d57accf-750c-4143-addd-8dbbeb124299">
  <data key="d3">The monthly COI is calculated based on the gap between the death benefit and the account value, using a specific rate applied per thousand dollars. Since the account value may fluctuate—but remains protected by a 0% floor—it influences how much COI is charged without affecting the rule defining the floor.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT OF</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="749f8b25-e9c6-4e2e-a60a-5fdcef1215c4" target="116850af-15ce-4679-bcc4-58d5753875d7">
  <data key="d3">The COI charge depends on the difference between the death benefit and current account value, calculated via a fixed formula. Since this charge reduces the account value, it may affect how much the account grows despite a zero-interest scenario after a 5% decline.</data>
  <data key="d4">MODIFIES ACCOUNT VALUE</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.45</data>
</edge>
