  <data key="d3">The method used to calculate the monthly COI has no bearing on when premiums may be paid, as it pertains only to ongoing fees based on policy values.</data>
  <data key="d4">NO RELATIONSHIP</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="749f8b25-e9c6-4e2e-a60a-5fdcef1215c4" target="86b72d75-5a59-44c2-b2d6-912ce393cf76">
  <data key="d3">The method used to calculate the monthly COI charge has no bearing on whether premiums can be paid at any time; it operates independently of payment scheduling.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="749f8b25-e9c6-4e2e-a60a-5fdcef1215c4" target="7d3930c1-6b23-4a04-93b3-d874c9e7b176">
  <data key="d3">The COI rate determines how much is charged monthly based on risk exposure, influencing overall policy cost—but does not affect the required minimum premium amount set at $100 per payment.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT OF</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="749f8b25-e9c6-4e2e-a60a-5fdcef1215c4" target="44171d39-27ff-4fcc-bf40-58a3e92080fa">
  <data key="d3">The monthly COI rate calculation relies on the current account value, which is influenced by regular premium payments—including those at the minimum level of $100—thereby linking the cost mechanism back to basic payment requirements.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="749f8b25-e9c6-4e2e-a60a-5fdcef1215c4" target="b8046f06-ce88-4546-bb7c-16ba4c69bcf2">
  <data key="d3">The calculation method for monthly COI depends on the difference between the death benefit and account value, regardless of the premium cap mentioned in fact_sentence_1. The two facts describe separate operational rules without mutual influence.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="749f8b25-e9c6-4e2e-a60a-5fdcef1215c4" target="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2">
  <data key="d3">The COI formula depends on the difference between the death benefit and current account value, scaled by a rate. Since higher premiums up to $50,000 increase the account value, they reduce future COI costs, making the premium cap relevant to long-term affordability.</data>
  <data key="d4">MODULATES_COSTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
