<edge source="e1d7d59c-ecc5-48bc-b7c4-1fd4fcfbe199" target="ec56b365-0020-450d-9cc6-283a79189906">
  <data key="d3">Option 1's designation as offering a level death benefit means the payout remains unchanged throughout the policy term, specifically set at $500,000.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEFINED_BY</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e1d7d59c-ecc5-48bc-b7c4-1fd4fcfbe199" target="1ff5fee4-2e90-43f5-9b90-379cb6250678">
  <data key="d3">Option 1 offers a fixed death benefit regardless of account performance, contrasting with Option 2, which increases the payout based on accumulated account value alongside the base specification.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONTRASTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e1d7d59c-ecc5-48bc-b7c4-1fd4fcfbe199" target="f48d18db-916a-4e9a-ad5c-8b176c59da3c">
  <data key="d3">The level death benefit feature defines the payout structure of Option 1, whereas its lower ongoing charges reflect reduced expenses over time. Together, they highlight key financial characteristics of the plan.</data>
  <data key="d4">COMPLEMENTARY</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e1d7d59c-ecc5-48bc-b7c4-1fd4fcfbe199" target="9dd93a2e-b1e4-427c-b771-0da3121d9f76">
  <data key="d3">Option 1 defines a stable death benefit amount, providing a baseline feature that enables the possibility of switching to other options like the increasing type.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENABLES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e1d7d59c-ecc5-48bc-b7c4-1fd4fcfbe199" target="3d7d5200-92e7-4186-968d-e19ab1fbc8b6">
  <data key="d3">Option 1 offers a constant death benefit amount, providing predictability, whereas Option 2 allows the death benefit to grow dynamically based on account performance, offering higher long-term potential but less stability.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONTRASTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e1d7d59c-ecc5-48bc-b7c4-1fd4fcfbe199" target="99ca8905-b57d-4689-9164-12678eda198b">
  <data key="d3">Option 1 maintains a constant death benefit amount over time, providing predictability in payouts. This contrasts with Option 2, which incurs higher ongoing charges, potentially affecting long-term policy performance.</data>
  <data key="d4">COMPARATIVE DIFFERENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e1d7d59c-ecc5-48bc-b7c4-1fd4fcfbe199" target="52cae3fa-7111-45dd-93a0-0249fc2b3e25">
  <data key="d3">The presence of a Level death benefit option enables policyholders to consider switching plans, making the requirement for advance notice applicable when modifying such benefits.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENABLES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="e1d7d59c-ecc5-48bc-b7c4-1fd4fcfbe199" target="3493040d-88ba-470b-9604-c6e5ce8f6501">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a level death benefit under Option 1 does not influence or relate to the requirement for evidence of insurability when choosing higher coverage under the increasing option.</data>
  <data key="d4">NO RELATIONSHIP</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
