  <data key="d3">The 7% surrender charge in Year 4 reflects a penalty for withdrawing funds before maturity, unrelated to the S&amp;P 500's 0% floor mechanism, which protects account value from negative returns.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5935cab6-342e-4e3e-9ea8-dbff499af332" target="a0671d34-e31b-4b17-8ed5-6a9d495c5148">
  <data key="d3">A 7% surrender charge in Year 4 affects liquidity and potential exit strategies, whereas the one-year crediting period determines how gains are measured over time. Together, they influence when it may be financially optimal to access funds versus letting compounding occur.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT_FEATURE</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.25</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5935cab6-342e-4e3e-9ea8-dbff499af332" target="62714aed-4640-40fa-9927-1e7eaaddb24c">
  <data key="d3">The Year 4 surrender charge reflects a contractual penalty for withdrawing funds early, which may impact decisions about utilizing returns generated by index accounts like the NASDAQ-100. However, it does not alter or depend on the specific characteristics of those indexes.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
