  <data key="d3">A 7% surrender charge in Year 4 reflects early exit cost, whereas the 9.0% cap on the NASDAQ-100 Index Account governs upside growth potential. Neither directly influences nor depends on the other.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5935cab6-342e-4e3e-9ea8-dbff499af332" target="32909f74-b806-4169-8ab1-602ea158ef9f">
  <data key="d3">The 7% surrender charge in Year 4 reflects a penalty for early termination, whereas the 100% participation rate determines return generation based on index performance. There is no causal or conditional link between these two attributes.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5935cab6-342e-4e3e-9ea8-dbff499af332" target="bce094ed-5c85-40d1-800b-e0894835715b">
  <data key="d3">A 7% surrender charge applies in Year 4 regardless of the NASDAQ-100 Index Account’s performance, which includes its 0% floor that protects against losses. This means even if the index performs poorly, the policyholder still faces a fee upon withdrawal.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
