</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="e27b0a94-4088-4246-ba72-d6793b6cd04d">
  <data key="d3">Dividend exclusion during negative index performance does not affect the ability to use the 61-day grace period to restore coverage after a lapse. The two provisions apply to separate policy features.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="61eb2693-c254-4b76-aefb-4ce00bdc5d2b">
  <data key="d3">Even if the index declines and no dividends are distributed, it does not affect the requirement to pay premiums or trigger policy lapse unless the grace period expires without payment.</data>
  <data key="d4"></data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="15cc61a2-a432-4cb5-8906-a25d63f7e7f6">
  <data key="d3">The second statement clarifies that negative index movements do not trigger dividend distributions, whereas the first outlines the repayment of surrender value at lapse. These represent independent provisions concerning benefit disbursement under differing circumstances.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="f059bbb2-2661-4ab8-9504-a5a0a25687f5">
  <data key="d3">The second sentence describes how negative index performance affects dividend eligibility, whereas the first sentence defines payout rules for suicide deaths within a specified timeframe. Neither statement depends on nor alters the meaning of the other, as they address unrelated conditions in the policy.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="66d61507-a301-4354-ad15-acfad1eafccb">
  <data key="d3">Negative index performance means the policyholder forfeits dividend eligibility, regardless of other benefits. This contrasts with the first statement, which defines a limited payout scenario for suicide-related claims during a specified window.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="1fb961d9-6574-4554-b263-906d75b3f515">
  <data key="d3">Negative index performance results in no dividend participation for the policyholder, regardless of whether the policy was issued with any material misrepresentations; this condition stands independently of the contestability clause.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="6e42de55-cde0-435f-b499-10c6b12172e2">
  <data key="d3">The absence of dividend payouts when index performance is negative reflects one aspect of how the policy manages risk exposure, which aligns with broader disclosure principles emphasized by stating it is not a securities product.</data>
  <data key="d4">INFORMS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="d95da1e8-f071-414b-b0ad-9b56d2f0e759">
  <data key="d3">The exclusion of dividend participation during negative index movements stems from the fact that index participation is not based on real equity holdings but rather on calculated rate adjustments.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="2ec53e58-1461-418f-af0f-33f4dab5f971">
  <data key="d3">The absence of dividend participation during negative index movements stems directly from the lack of direct ownership in index funds, which defines how index-linked policies operate.</data>
  <data key="d4">CAUSED_BY</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="6d5cb567-6ee5-41f3-8c36-a83d2ff5c7a3">
  <data key="d3">The absence of dividend participation during negative index performance stems from the design where such dividends are explicitly excluded from index performance metrics, ensuring they don’t influence payout outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d4">RESULT_OF</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="a6b58496-e890-407d-99f3-f8e86cce69dd">
  <data key="d3">Negative index performance results in no dividend distribution to the policyholder, regardless of any existing cap rate limitations set for positive performance periods.</data>
  <data key="d4">LIMITS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="7e3f4dce-a967-443e-8c1e-f4c1a24f502c">
  <data key="d3">Negative index performance eliminates dividend participation entirely, rendering changes in annual participation rates irrelevant during such periods.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONDITIONS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="09f59f75-d8f5-4cf9-b763-8e9b77770ca0">
  <data key="d3">Dividend exclusion during negative index performance reflects how the policy handles poor market results. This rule operates independently of any decisions about fixed account rate adjustments, which occur separately based on insurer discretion.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="b080d9ad-b053-459f-8150-83b862b99de1">
  <data key="d3">Negative index performance results in no dividend participation, though this limitation is balanced by the presence of guaranteed minimum rates that prevent total loss.</data>
  <data key="d4">LIMITED_BY</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="a416fa11-2b3f-4f38-958d-ff8e13bcbb1a">
  <data key="d3">Negative index performance triggers exclusion from dividend participation; however, the policy's guaranteed minimums prevent any reduction in participation rates below a set threshold.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONDITIONAL_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="af34274c-dfee-4fb4-a491-3e2e93de0364">
  <data key="d3">Negative index performance results in no dividend participation, whereas the fixed account maintains its guaranteed minimum rates regardless of such events. The two clauses serve distinct protective functions without mutual reliance.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENTLY PROTECTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.35</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="5ba693f1-db68-45ea-a535-eef1cf763796">
  <data key="d3">Negative index performance directly leads to exclusion from dividend participation, reinforcing the idea that even consistent historical gains cannot ensure ongoing benefits, aligning with the caution about non-guaranteed future outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d4">EXPLAINS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="47fe3387-29bc-4869-a3a5-637bc22b6a91">
  <data key="d3">When the index declines, the policyholder avoids losses because they don't receive dividends, reinforcing the concept that zero represents safety. This outcome justifies calling zero the “hero” in risk protection.</data>
  <data key="d4">SUPPORTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="05f6ec15-bbb6-4acf-8ed9-90e66f40d069">
  <data key="d3">Since the policyholder does not participate in dividends when index performance is negative, this contributes to the overall design that prevents any downside risk, ensuring they cannot lose money.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONTRIBUTES_TO</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="b1733959-f2c8-49a1-abbf-c49d75d321d4">
  <data key="d3">The first sentence explains how dividend distribution is affected by negative index movements. The second sentence gives general contact information for the insurance provider, which has no bearing on the dividend mechanism described earlier.</data>
  <data key="d4">NONE</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3" target="87987c98-b6f9-450a-8c71-84809999dd2b">
  <data key="d3">The first sentence explains how negative index performance affects dividend eligibility under the policy. The second sentence gives general contact details for the insurance provider, which has no bearing on the dividend rules described earlier.</data>
  <data key="d4">NONE</data>
